AHS-001 — Purpose & Scope

Document ID: AHS-001
Title: Purpose & Scope
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

The Atlas Hivemind Specification (AHS) defines the canonical system architecture of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

While the Atlas Core Concepts (ACC) establish the normative conceptual foundation of engineering cognition, AHS specifies how those concepts are organized into a coherent, interoperable, and extensible cognitive architecture.

AHS provides the architectural blueprint governing the structure, interaction, responsibilities, and lifecycle of Atlas components. It ensures that implementations remain consistent with the canonical concepts defined by ACC while enabling scalable engineering cognition across diverse domains and deployment environments.

2. Objectives

The objectives of this specification are to:

Define the canonical architecture of Atlas.
Organize engineering cognition into well-defined architectural layers and components.
Specify the responsibilities and interactions of architectural elements.
Establish standardized information and control flows.
Enable modular, scalable, and interoperable implementations.
Ensure architectural consistency across all Atlas specifications and implementations.
Provide a stable foundation for future extensions without altering the conceptual model defined by ACC.
3. Scope

This specification defines the architecture of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework, including:

System architecture
Architectural layers
Cognitive components
Service boundaries
Information flow
Control flow
Component interactions
Architectural constraints
Architectural quality attributes
Conformance requirements

The specification is independent of any programming language, runtime, storage technology, communication protocol, or deployment model.

4. Out of Scope

This specification does not define:

Canonical engineering concepts (see ACC)
Domain-specific engineering knowledge
Algorithms or implementation details
User interface design
Programming language APIs
Database schemas
Machine learning models
Deployment configurations
Infrastructure technologies

These topics are addressed by other Atlas specifications or implementation documents.

5. Relationship to Atlas Core Concepts

AHS is founded upon the normative concepts defined by the Atlas Core Concepts (ACC).

All architectural elements described by this specification SHALL conform to the canonical definitions, relationships, and constraints established by ACC.

AHS extends ACC by defining how canonical concepts are organized and coordinated within a system architecture. It does not modify or redefine any concept established by ACC.

6. Architectural Vision

The Atlas architecture is designed to enable engineering cognition through a modular system of cooperating components that transform engineering observations into explainable, evidence-based decision support.

The architecture emphasizes:

Conceptual integrity
Separation of concerns
Explainability
Traceability
Modularity
Extensibility
Technology independence
Standards-based interoperability
Human-centered engineering decision support
7. Intended Audience

This specification is intended for:

System architects
Software architects
AI and cognitive system engineers
Engineering platform developers
Knowledge engineers
Standards authors
Integration engineers
Researchers implementing Atlas-compliant systems
8. Normative References

This specification SHALL be interpreted in conjunction with:

ACC-001 through ACC-022 (Atlas Core Concepts)

Future Atlas specifications (e.g., EUS, EMS, EKS, ERS, ECS, DSS) are informative unless explicitly referenced as normative.

9. Conformance

An implementation conforms to AHS if it:

Preserves the canonical concepts defined by ACC.
Implements the architectural principles and constraints defined by AHS.
Maintains architectural consistency across components.
Supports the required information and control flows.
Does not introduce architectural contradictions.
10. Future Evolution

Future revisions of AHS MAY introduce:

Additional architectural views
New component types
New interaction patterns
New deployment models
Additional quality attributes

provided they remain consistent with the normative concepts defined by ACC.